\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The evidence hierarchy is robust to several potential confounds, though important caveats remain.

\paragraph{Topic shortcut confound.}
Three observations argue against a topic-level shortcut: (1) topic classification yields unstable OOD generalization (F1\,=\,0.541$\pm$0.207 on DD); (2) scrambled labels match correct labels (F1 gap $\leq$3.9\,pp); (3) MLM achieves strong detection without labels (F1\,$\geq$\,0.97 at $K \leq 5$). Domain exposure, not classification, drives detection.

\paragraph{Single-LLM confound.}
Both training and synthetic OOD data are generated by Qwen3-8B. ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (204 human-written jailbreaks) provides partial resolution: all domain-adapted variants outperform vanilla by 18--42\,pp (Table~\ref{tab:human_data}). A cross-LLM experiment (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_llm}) further isolates the confound: with Llama-3.1-8B jailbreaks and Qwen benign text, detection F1 recovers to 0.80--1.00; the remaining ActorAttack gap (0.80 vs.\ 1.00) reflects genuine cross-LLM variation. Multi-turn human data is needed to confirm the complete hierarchy.

\paragraph{Variance reversal on human data.}
On synthetic OOD, classification supervision reduces cross-seed variance 4--20$\times$ over MLM; on ToxicChat, this reverses (9-class std\,=\,0.087 vs.\ JB-MLM 0.013). This pattern suggests that classification supervision overfits to distributional regularities in the training data, while MLM captures more transferable token-level features.

\paragraph{Encoder capacity confound.}
DeBERTa-v3-base (184M), RoBERTa-base (125M), and RoBERTa-large (355M) span a 2.8$\times$ capacity range but also differ in pretraining objectives. The consistent capacity gradient across three models (Section~\ref{sec:cross_encoder}) suggests that model size is the dominant factor, though we cannot fully rule out architectural contributions.

